In Rogue the emphasis seems to me to be shifted away from finishing a
quest, as in NetHack, and more toward getting a high score. I don't
think it was made to be impossible, but the programming is so dependant
on random numbers that it is theoretically possible to walk straight to
the amulet without much resistance at all. In the commercial version of
Rogue sold by Epyx there were even changes to the first dungeon level
that eased the difficulty.

Rogue was a very early entry in the history of computer games, and most
of the people who had access to it at that time were expecting a
devilishly difficult experience. Anything less would have caused the
game to seem trivial, programmers would have found the Roguelike genre
unappealing, and maybe Hack and NetHack would have never been made!

There are definitely strategies that can be applied to Rogue, and if
the random number generator cooperates, a player's chances will be
significantly better. I once saw a guide to winning Rogue somewhere on
the Web (can't remember where), which was written by someone who had
won more than once. In short, it is possible to win, and to increase
your chances, plan your moves carefully. Avoid fights you can't win;
retreating can be risky, but better than dying. There will be games in
which you can't find food, or never find good armor, etc., so just quit
and try again. Use every item you find to its fullest extent, and keep
some items for insurance in case of dire circumstances.


